<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!DOCTYPE fontconfig SYSTEM "urn:fontconfig:fonts.dtd">
<!--
  The machine, with no Geist on it.

  This suite proves the console fetches Geist and renders in it. That proof is
  worthless on a developer machine that has Geist INSTALLED — and this one does,
  and so does anyone else's who has ever installed it. With a local Geist the
  renderer resolves the family without a single byte crossing the wire,
  `CSS.getPlatformFontsForNode` reports "Geist", and every assertion in
  e2e/font.spec.ts passes against a build with no webfont at all. That is the
  precise shape of the bug this whole change exists to close: a font that looks
  right locally and is a serif in production.

  So Chromium is pointed at this fontconfig instead of the machine's, and it
  names only the distribution's font directory. /usr/local/share/fonts, where a
  hand-installed Geist lands, is not in it, so the browser has no way to obtain
  the face except the one production uses.

  This file is not taken on trust. `the face on screen came over the wire` in
  e2e/font.spec.ts blocks the CDN and asserts the console is then NOT in Geist:
  if a local copy were still visible, that test fails, and it is the reason this
  one can be believed.

  It stays a font environment rather than a stripped one: the distribution's own
  rules are included, so the fallbacks the stacks name (`system-ui`,
  `ui-monospace`, `monospace`) resolve to what they resolve to anywhere else.
  What is isolated is Geist and nothing else.
-->
<fontconfig>
  <dir>/usr/share/fonts</dir>
  <cachedir prefix="xdg">fontconfig</cachedir>
  <include ignore_missing="yes">/etc/fonts/conf.d</include>
</fontconfig>
